I"©<p>Making art with the AxiDraw is immediately rewarding. Hearing the whirr of the servos as your art is drawn right before my eyes will never get old. I feel a strong urge to get an SVG finished so I can start playing with paper and markers.</p>

<p>Later, the finished plots start to pile up. The design which was so engrossing while being plotted looks ordinary and uninteresting once the pen stops moving. It looks like wallpaper. The entire page full of intricate pattern and detail blurs together before my eyes. Why do so many plots end up that way?</p>

<p>Part of it are the inherent limitations and advantages of pen plotting. Any form of mechanical reproduction can make more images than an artist working by hand. This advantage leads to designs rich in detail. But repetitive detail turns into pattern which quickly turns into wallpaper. The SVG format is also to blame: most of us donâ€™t think in vector but in raster.</p>
